#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Register Builder's MCP servers, and read back what was actually registered.

    bin/register-mcp              register both servers
    bin/register-mcp --status     what is registered, and whether it connects
    bin/register-mcp --dry-run    say what would happen, change nothing
    bin/register-mcp --check      report only; 1 if anything is missing or wrong

**A SESSION WITH NO MCP SERVERS HAS NO REFERENCE ROOM AND NO WORK QUEUE**, and
until this existed nothing installed them and nothing said they were missing.
`bin/install-backend`'s `outstanding()` named supervision, the gateway
credentials file and per-repository path conversion, and never mentioned this —
so an operator who followed every line the installer printed ended with a
machine whose agents could not reach the Library, and no output told them.
The instructions lived in `agent-platform/install.sh`'s closing text, which is
prose in a shell comment, which is where they stayed.

## `claude mcp add` CANNOT REPAIR A WRONG ENTRY, AND SAYS SO THE SAME WAY IT
## SAYS EVERYTHING IS FINE

This is the trap the whole file is shaped around, measured against the real CLI:

    claude mcp add --scope user ops -- /fake/mcp-ops-server   # first time, 0
    claude mcp add --scope user ops -- /fake/mcp-ops-server   # identical
    → "MCP server ops already exists in user config", EXIT 1
    claude mcp add --scope user ops -- /a/completely/other/path
    → "MCP server ops already exists in user config", EXIT 1,
      and the entry STILL points at /fake/mcp-ops-server

**The two cases are indistinguishable from outside.** A re-run against a machine
that is already correct and a re-run against one registered with a stale path
produce the same message and the same exit code, and in the second case the
command has silently declined to fix the thing it was asked to fix. An installer
branching on that code would either treat a harmless no-op as a failure or treat
a real conflict as "already done"; there is no reading of it that is right in
both cases.

A check whose passing and failing states produce identical evidence is not a
check. **So nothing here believes an exit code. Every run reads the registration
back out of the config and compares it to what was intended**, which is the same
reason `bin/provision-backend` executes every program it installs rather than
trusting apt's exit code.

This matters concretely: on the machine this was built on, `ops` pointed into a
live working tree, so
a naive installer would report "already exists" over exactly the registration
that most needs replacing.

## THE DEFAULT SCOPE IS `local`, AND `local` MEANS ONE DIRECTORY

`agent-platform/install.sh` documents the registration as:

    claude mcp add kb -- ~/.local/bin/mcp-kb-server

`--scope` defaults to `local`, and a local registration is written under
`projects.<cwd>.mcpServers` in `~/.claude.json` — scoped to the directory the
command ran in. Measured against a throwaway HOME: that exact command leaves
top-level `mcpServers` empty and the entry under the working directory. An
operator following the documented procedure on a fresh machine registers the
servers for whichever directory they happened to be standing in, and every crew
session started anywhere else has no Library.

**`--scope user` is the only correct scope here** and is what puts the entry at
top-level `mcpServers`, which is the shape a working install has.

## WHY THIS WRITES THROUGH THE CLI AND READS THROUGH THE FILE

`~/.claude.json` is written by every running Claude session, continuously — it
holds per-project session state, not just this registration. A read-modify-write
of that file from here would race every live session and could drop whatever
they wrote in between, so **this never writes it.** `claude mcp add` is the
supported surface and owns that concurrency.

Reading it is a different matter and is exact, so the registration facts — the
name, the scope, the command, the environment — are read from the JSON rather
than parsed out of console output. Only one fact cannot be got that way.

## HEALTH IS READ FROM `Status:`, BECAUSE THE EXIT CODE CANNOT SEE IT

    claude mcp get <a working server>   → exit 0, "Status: ✔ Connected"
    claude mcp get <a broken server>    → exit 0, "Status: ✘ Failed to connect"

Both measured. The exit code distinguishes *configured* from *not configured*
and never *healthy* from *broken*, so a check written on it would produce
identical evidence in its passing and failing states. The `Status:` line is the
only place the difference appears, and an unrecognised status is reported as
`unknown` rather than guessed in either direction.

## It writes no credentials

The `ops` server needs an agent-class token at `~/.orient-control/ops-mcp-token`.
The Library's job declaration mints it, once, the first time the install prepares
the Library, and never replaces it. This command only registers servers.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

PROG = "register-mcp"

# Where a service is allowed to serve from: a deploy area, never a checkout
# anybody develops in. `bin/install-backend` places `agent-platform`
# here, and this points the registrations at that copy rather than at
# `~/.local/bin/mcp-*-server`, which on a development machine is a symlink into the live
# working tree — so every session's MCP subprocess runs whatever branch was
# last left checked out there.
DEFAULT_DEPLOY_ROOT = Path.home() / ".local" / "deploy"

# The servers, what each is for, and where inside a deploy area it lives.
#
# NEITHER CARRIES AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE, and that is a decision rather than an
# omission. An older `ops` entry carries `VAULTS_ON_LIBRARY=1`, which was needed
# while the vault plane still defaulted to Orient. That default was deliberately
# inverted after the cutover — `agent-platform/lib/agent-platform/vault_plane.py`
# states that unset now means the Library, because a default meaning "the store
# we migrated away from" needed 75 correct edits to be safe and one omission to
# be wrong. Unset is now the safe answer, so carrying the variable forward would
# be copying a value because one machine happens to have it.
SERVERS = (
    {
        "name": "kb",
        "why": "the Reference Room — every session's knowledge tools",
        "relative": "agent-platform/bin/mcp-kb-server",
        "env": {},
    },
    {
        "name": "ops",
        "why": "the work queue, signals and the vaults",
        "relative": "agent-platform/bin/mcp-ops-server",
        "env": {},
    },
)


class Refused(Exception):
    """Something the operator has to decide about. Never a stack trace."""


def run(argv: list[str], **kwargs) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    return subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, **kwargs)


def claude_cli() -> str:
    """The `claude` binary, or a refusal naming what to do about it."""
    found = shutil.which("claude")
    if found is None:
        raise Refused(
            "`claude` is not on PATH, and it is what registers an MCP server. "
            "Install Claude Code first; nothing was registered."
        )
    return found


def config_path() -> Path:
    """The config file THE CLI WILL ACTUALLY WRITE, not merely `~/.claude.json`.

    `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` relocates it, and this was measured rather than assumed:
    with that variable set, `claude mcp add --scope user` writes
    `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json` and leaves `$HOME/.claude.json` absent.

    **Reading the wrong file here would make the whole design lie.** This tool
    decides what is registered, and confirms its own writes, by reading this
    path; pointed at `$HOME` on a machine that relocates the config, it would
    see nothing registered, run `add`, read back nothing again, and refuse with
    "still not registered as intended" over a registration that had in fact
    succeeded. It fails closed, which is the right direction and still wrong.

    HOME comes from the environment rather than `Path.home()` for the same
    reason: a test points the CLI and this at one throwaway directory.
    """
    relocated = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR")
    if relocated:
        return Path(relocated).expanduser() / ".claude.json"
    return Path(os.environ.get("HOME", str(Path.home()))) / ".claude.json"


def registered_servers() -> dict:
    """The user-scope registrations, read from the file rather than from output.

    A missing or unreadable file means nothing is registered, which is the true
    answer on a fresh machine. A file that exists and does not parse is a
    refusal: something else owns that file and this must not write over it.
    """
    path = config_path()
    if not path.exists():
        return {}
    try:
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"{path} cannot be read ({exc}).")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as exc:
        # A TORN READ IS THE CASE TO EXPECT HERE, not an exotic one: every live
        # session writes this file continuously, so catching it one frame away
        # from the JSON error — where it would surface as a traceback at exit 1,
        # the same code `--check` uses for "not registered as intended" — is the
        # difference between a refusal and a crash that reads like a verdict.
        raise Refused(
            f"{path} is not valid UTF-8 ({exc}), which a read torn by a "
            f"concurrent write looks like. Try again."
        )
    if not text.strip():
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads(text)
    except (ValueError, RecursionError) as exc:
        # RecursionError is a RuntimeError rather than a ValueError, so it
        # escapes a bare `except ValueError` and comes out as a traceback.
        raise Refused(
            f"{path} is not valid JSON ({exc}). This will not write over a file "
            f"it cannot read."
        )
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        raise Refused(f"{path} does not hold a JSON object.")
    servers = data.get("mcpServers")
    return servers if isinstance(servers, dict) else {}


def health(cli: str, name: str) -> str:
    """`connected`, `broken`, or `unknown` — read from `Status:`, never the code.

    `claude mcp get` exits 0 for a working server and 0 for one that cannot
    start, so the exit code is evidence of being configured and of nothing else.
    An unrecognised status is `unknown`: reporting it as broken would invent a
    failure and as connected would hide one.
    """
    done = run([cli, "mcp", "get", name], timeout=120)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return "absent"
    for line in (done.stdout or "").splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if not stripped.startswith("Status:"):
            continue
        value = stripped[len("Status:"):].strip()
        # FAILURE MARKERS ARE TESTED FIRST, AND `connected` NEEDS THE EXACT
        # MEASURED FORM. Written the other way round — `"Connected" in value`
        # before the failure check — a status such as `✘ Not Connected` comes
        # back `connected`, which resolves an ambiguous string in the one
        # direction that hides a broken server. Only `✔ Connected` was
        # measured, so only it counts; everything else is `unknown`, which is
        # what this function claims to do with a string it does not recognise.
        if value.startswith("✘") or "Failed" in value:
            return "broken"
        if value == "✔ Connected":
            return "connected"
        return "unknown"
    return "unknown"


def env_keys(env) -> list[str]:
    """The NAMES of an entry's environment variables, never their values.

    `claude mcp add -e KEY=value` is the standard place an MCP server's
    credentials live, and these messages print an entry this tool did not write
    — so a value here is somebody's API key on the stderr of an installer whose
    output gets captured and pasted. The names are what the reader needs to see
    which entry is wrong; the values add nothing and carry the whole risk.
    """
    if not isinstance(env, dict):
        return []
    return sorted(env)


def brief(value, limit: int = 60) -> str:
    """A value's repr, cut to `limit` characters plus a truncation marker.

    **A SIZE LIMIT, NOT A REDACTION.** An earlier version of this docstring said
    the bound was "enough to identify it, not enough to dump it", which read as
    a promise about secrets. It is not one: `['--token=ghp_` and a 36-character
    token is about 52 characters and prints whole under the cap, and so do most
    API keys and URL passwords. What this bounds is how much of an entry this
    tool did not write reaches stderr, so a pasted refusal stays short. Whether
    a secret appears there is decided by what is passed in, which is why a
    malformed `env` is printed as its type rather than through this function.
    If a credential in `args` is ever seen in a pasted refusal, print each arg
    only up to its first `=` instead.

    `repr` also escapes control characters and newlines, so a crafted value
    cannot forge a second "should be:" line in this message.
    """
    text = repr(value)
    return text if len(text) <= limit else text[:limit] + "…(truncated)"


def intended(server: dict, deploy_root: Path) -> dict:
    return {
        "name": server["name"],
        "why": server["why"],
        "command": str(deploy_root / server["relative"]),
        "env": dict(server["env"]),
    }


def compare(want: dict, have: dict | None) -> str:
    """What state this registration is in, before anything is changed.

    `mismatch` is the state that matters, because it is the one `claude mcp add`
    will not repair.

    **Every field the registration carries is compared, not just the command.**
    A check narrower than the thing it claims to verify is the defect this file
    is otherwise about: `args` and `type` drift exactly as `env` does — the
    `VAULTS_ON_LIBRARY` left on an older `ops` entry is that drift already
    having happened — and an entry with the right command and a stray `args`
    would otherwise read `present` while the tool reported nothing to do.
    """
    if have is None:
        return "absent"
    # A NON-DICT ENTRY IS SOMEBODY ELSE'S IDEA OF THIS FILE, not ours to read
    # through. `{"ops": "x"}` otherwise raises AttributeError on the next line
    # and leaves at exit 1 with a traceback — which is the code `--check` uses
    # for "not registered as intended", so a crash would read as a verdict.
    if not isinstance(have, dict):
        return "mismatch"
    if have.get("command") != want["command"]:
        return "mismatch"
    have_env = have.get("env") or {}
    have_args = have.get("args") or []
    # Guarded for the same reason `have` itself is: a hand-edited `"env": ["x"]`
    # would otherwise raise inside a comparison, one frame from the `env_keys()`
    # that already defends the identical field.
    if not isinstance(have_env, dict) or not isinstance(have_args, list):
        return "mismatch"
    if dict(have_env) != want["env"]:
        return "mismatch"
    if have_args != []:
        return "mismatch"
    if have.get("type", "stdio") != "stdio":
        return "mismatch"
    return "present"


def plan(deploy_root: Path) -> list[dict]:
    """Every registration's intended and actual state, computed before any write.

    Computed up front so `--dry-run` and a real run answer the same question,
    and so a refusal names the whole picture rather than the first thing wrong.
    """
    have = registered_servers()
    items = []
    for server in SERVERS:
        want = intended(server, deploy_root)
        actual = have.get(want["name"])
        items.append({
            **want,
            "state": compare(want, actual),
            "actual": actual,
            "deployed": Path(want["command"]).exists(),
        })
    return items


def refuse_mismatches(items: list[dict]) -> None:
    """A wrong entry is the operator's to clear, because a re-add cannot.

    This is the whole reason the command reads its work back. Left to itself,
    `claude mcp add` would exit 0 here and change nothing.
    """
    wrong = [item for item in items if item["state"] == "mismatch"]
    if not wrong:
        return
    lines = [
        (
            "these servers are registered with something other than what "
            "belongs there, and `claude mcp add` will not repair them — it "
            "reports `already exists` and leaves them as they are:"
        )
    ]
    for item in wrong:
        actual = item["actual"] if isinstance(item["actual"], dict) else {}
        lines.append(f"  {item['name']}:")
        # SHOW WHICHEVER FIELD DIFFERS, for every class `compare()` can return.
        # Printing only the command and the env keys rendered a mismatch caused
        # by `args`, `type`, a malformed `env` or a non-dict entry as two
        # IDENTICAL lines under a heading saying they differ.
        extra_have, extra_want = "", ""
        # NOT `list(...)`: `compare()` deliberately calls a non-list `args` a
        # mismatch, so `"args": 5` reaches here, and `list(5)` raises TypeError
        # — a traceback at exit 1, which is the code `--check` uses for a
        # verdict. That crash-that-reads-like-a-verdict is the shape this file
        # exists to avoid, and the previous line reintroduced it one frame down.
        have_args = actual.get("args") or []
        if have_args != []:
            extra_have += f" args={brief(have_args)}"
            extra_want += " args=[]"
        if actual.get("type", "stdio") != "stdio":
            extra_have += f" type={brief(actual.get('type'))}"
            extra_want += " type='stdio'"
        have_env = actual.get("env") or {}
        if not isinstance(have_env, dict):
            # `env_keys` renders this as `[]`, which matches the intended empty
            # env exactly — so without this the two lines agree while the entry
            # is the reason for the refusal.
            #
            # THE TYPE, NEVER THE CONTENT. `env` is the one field `env_keys`
            # exists to keep off stderr, and a list-form env is exactly where
            # `["API_KEY=…"]` would sit, so printing it through `brief()` put
            # the value back. The type is what the operator needs to fix it.
            extra_have += f" env=<{type(have_env).__name__}>"
        if not isinstance(item["actual"], dict):
            extra_have += f" entry={brief(item['actual'])}"
        lines.append(
            f"    registered: {actual.get('command')!r} "
            f"env keys={env_keys(actual.get('env'))}{extra_have}"
        )
        lines.append(
            f"    should be:  {item['command']!r} "
            f"env keys={env_keys(item['env'])}{extra_want}"
        )
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Remove each one and run this again:")
    for item in wrong:
        lines.append(f"    claude mcp remove {item['name']} -s user")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Nothing was registered.")
    raise Refused("\n".join(lines))


def refuse_undeployed(items: list[dict]) -> None:
    """Registering a path that does not exist is accepted silently by the CLI.

    `claude mcp add --scope user missing -- /does/not/exist` exits 0 and writes
    the entry, so the registration would look installed and fail at every
    session start. The deploy area is what should exist first.
    """
    absent = [item for item in items if not item["deployed"]]
    if not absent:
        return
    lines = ["the deploy area does not hold these servers yet:"]
    for item in absent:
        lines.append(f"    {item['command']}")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append(
        "`claude mcp add` accepts a command that does not exist and exits 0, so "
        "registering now would look installed and fail at every session start. "
        "Place the backend first:"
    )
    lines.append("    bin/install-backend")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Nothing was registered.")
    raise Refused("\n".join(lines))


def register_one(cli: str, item: dict) -> None:
    """Add one server, then READ IT BACK, because the exit code proves nothing.

    **The exit code is deliberately not consulted at all.** `claude mcp add`
    exits 1 both when the entry is already exactly right and when it is present
    but wrong, so the code cannot separate the harmless case from the one that
    needs the operator. What settles it is the config afterwards: either the
    registration now says what it should, or it does not.
    """
    argv = [cli, "mcp", "add", "--scope", "user", item["name"]]
    for key, value in item["env"].items():
        argv += ["-e", f"{key}={value}"]
    argv += ["--", item["command"]]

    done = run(argv, timeout=120)

    # THE READ-BACK CAN ITSELF FAIL, AND IT RUNS AFTER THE WRITE. A torn read —
    # the case this file calls the one to expect, since every live session writes
    # that config — would otherwise surface through `registered_servers()`, whose
    # refusals used to end "Nothing was registered" over a server the `add` may
    # just have created. Same false-claim class as the half-registered comment.
    try:
        actual = registered_servers().get(item["name"])
    except Refused as exc:
        raise Refused(
            f"`claude mcp add` ran for {item['name']} and the read-back could "
            f"not confirm it: {exc}\nWhether it was registered is unknown; run "
            f"`{PROG} --check` to find out."
        ) from exc
    if compare(item, actual) != "present":
        found = actual if isinstance(actual, dict) else {}
        raise Refused(
            f"{item['name']} is still not registered as intended after "
            f"`claude mcp add` (which exited {done.returncode}).\n"
            f"    wanted: {item['command']!r} env keys={env_keys(item['env'])}\n"
            f"    found:  {found.get('command')!r} "
            f"env keys={env_keys(found.get('env'))}\n"
            f"    said:   {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()[-200:]!r}\n"
            f"Nothing further was attempted."
        )


def report(items: list[dict], cli: str, changed: list[str], probe: bool) -> None:
    print(f"{PROG}: user scope, {config_path()}")
    for item in items:
        mark = "registered" if item["name"] in changed else item["state"]
        line = f"  {mark:<11} {item['name']:<5} {item['why']}"
        if probe:
            line += f"  [{health(cli, item['name'])}]"
        print(line)
    print()
    # THE "NOTHING TO DO" LINE IS ABOUT THE PLAN, NOT ABOUT `changed`. Keyed on
    # an empty `changed` it also printed under `--dry-run`, `--check` and
    # `--status` on a machine with absent or mismatched servers — so the output
    # said "every server was already registered as intended" and then, two lines
    # later, "would register: kb, ops". A report that contradicts itself is
    # what this output exists to prevent.
    if changed:
        print(f"registered {len(changed)}: {', '.join(changed)}")
    elif all(item["state"] == "present" for item in items):
        print("nothing to do — every server was already registered as intended")


def outstanding() -> list[str]:
    """What this cannot do, named with the exact command or file.

    On stdout rather than in a comment, because the procedure this replaces
    ended with its manual steps in its closing text and that is where they
    stayed.
    """
    return [
        (
            "The ops server's token is ~/.orient-control/ops-mcp-token, which "
            "the Library's install step mints once and never replaces."
        ),
        "",
        (
            "A server shows `broken` until its own dependencies are met. Ask it "
            "directly rather than guessing, since the exit code of `get` is 0 "
            "either way:"
        ),
        "    claude mcp get ops",
        "",
        (
            "A registration already in place with the wrong command is the "
            "operator's to clear, because `claude mcp add` will not repair it "
            "— it reports `already exists` and changes nothing:"
        ),
        "    claude mcp remove <name> -s user",
        "",
        (
            "Sessions read their MCP registration when they spawn, so one "
            "already running does not pick this up. Start a new session to use "
            "what was just registered."
        ),
    ]


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG,
        description="Register Builder's MCP servers from a deploy area, at user scope.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--deploy", dest="deploy_root", type=Path, default=None,
        help="deploy root (default: ~/.local/deploy, or $BUILDER_DEPLOY_ROOT)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--status", action="store_true",
        help="what is registered and whether it connects, changing nothing",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--dry-run", action="store_true", help="say what would happen, change nothing"
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--check", action="store_true",
        help="report only, and exit 1 if anything is missing or wrong",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    try:
        cli = claude_cli()

        # `Path(os.environ.get(NAME, ""))` is `PosixPath('.')`, which is TRUTHY,
        # so the obvious default silently resolves the deploy root to the
        # working directory. The environment value is taken only when it is a
        # non-empty string.
        from_env = os.environ.get("BUILDER_DEPLOY_ROOT")
        if args.deploy_root is not None:
            deploy_root = args.deploy_root
        elif from_env:
            deploy_root = Path(from_env)
        else:
            deploy_root = DEFAULT_DEPLOY_ROOT

        # A RELATIVE ROOT IS WRITTEN INTO A MACHINE-WIDE CONFIG, WHICH IS THE
        # FAILURE THIS TOOL EXISTS TO PREVENT, ONE LEVEL DOWN. `--deploy
        # ./deploy` registers `deploy/agent-platform/bin/mcp-kb-server`; it
        # passes the existence check (resolved against THIS run's working
        # directory) and passes the read-back (the same relative string on both
        # sides), so nothing here would fail — and every session started from
        # another directory then cannot find the server, which is exactly the
        # per-directory breakage the module docstring warns about for `local`
        # scope. `bin/install-backend` refuses the same way.
        deploy_root = deploy_root.expanduser()
        if not deploy_root.is_absolute():
            raise Refused(
                f"the deploy root must be an absolute path: {deploy_root}\n"
                f"A relative path is resolved against whatever directory this "
                f"run happened to start in, and the registration it writes is "
                f"read by every session from every directory. Nothing was "
                f"registered."
            )

        # This read precedes every write, so a refusal from it CAN promise
        # that nothing was registered — which the reader itself cannot.
        try:
            items = plan(deploy_root)
        except Refused as exc:
            raise Refused(f"{exc}\nNothing was registered.") from exc

        if args.status:
            report(items, cli, changed=[], probe=True)
            return 0

        unready = [i for i in items if i["state"] != "present"]

        if args.check or args.dry_run:
            report(items, cli, changed=[], probe=False)
            # A PREDICTION THAT DISAGREES WITH THE RUN IS WORSE THAN NO
            # PREDICTION. Written as "would register everything not present",
            # `--dry-run` printed `would register: kb, ops` and exited 0 for a
            # mismatched entry or an unplaced backend — the two states where a
            # real run refuses and registers nothing. Those are exactly the
            # machine states this tool was built for, and `--dry-run` is what a
            # careful operator runs first to find them. So the refusals are
            # evaluated here too, and their own message is the prediction.
            refusal = None
            try:
                refuse_mismatches(items)
                refuse_undeployed(items)
            except Refused as exc:
                refusal = exc
            if refusal is not None:
                print(f"\na real run would refuse — {refusal}")
            elif unready:
                verb = "would register" if args.dry_run else "not registered as intended"
                print(f"\n{verb}: {', '.join(i['name'] for i in unready)}")
            print()
            for line in outstanding():
                print(line)
            if args.check and (unready or refusal is not None):
                return 1
            return 0

        # Both refusals run over the WHOLE plan before any write, so nothing is
        # registered when either fires. **That is not the same as ruling out a
        # partly-registered machine**: the loop below writes one server at a
        # time, so an `add` that fails its read-back — or a kill between the two
        # — leaves the servers already done registered and the rest absent. The
        # refusal below names what was written, and a re-run completes it.
        refuse_mismatches(items)
        refuse_undeployed(items)

        changed: list[str] = []
        for item in items:
            if item["state"] == "absent":
                try:
                    register_one(cli, item)
                except Refused as exc:
                    # SAY WHAT WAS ALREADY WRITTEN. Without this the refusal
                    # ends "Nothing further was attempted", which is true and
                    # reads as "nothing was written" — and the servers done
                    # before this one ARE registered.
                    if changed:
                        raise Refused(
                            f"{exc}\n\nAlready registered before this failed, "
                            f"and left in place: {', '.join(changed)}. Running "
                            f"this again completes the rest."
                        ) from exc
                    raise
                changed.append(item["name"])

        # Re-planned rather than assumed. The report says what IS, which is the
        # only thing worth printing after a run claiming to have changed things.
        # A failure of this LAST read must not lose what was registered: the
        # work succeeded, only the confirming re-read did not.
        try:
            report(plan(deploy_root), cli, changed, probe=False)
        except Refused as exc:
            # NAMES A NEXT STEP, because this path skips outstanding(). An
            # operator who has just registered both servers would otherwise see
            # neither the ops-token instructions nor the "start a new session"
            # line, and `--check` prints both.
            print(f"{PROG}: registered {', '.join(changed) or 'nothing'}, but the "
                  f"confirming re-read failed: {exc}\n"
                  f"Run `{PROG} --check` to confirm; it also prints the "
                  f"remaining steps.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        print()
        for line in outstanding():
            print(line)
        return 0

    except Refused as exc:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
